  <data key="d5">1778710103</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2</data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Glucose Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Glucose Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) is a technology that enables ongoing, real-time tracking of glucose levels using a small sensor inserted beneath the skin to measure glucose in the interstitial fluid. The sensor captures readings every few minutes, providing continuous data throughout the day and night, which offers a more comprehensive picture of glucose trends compared to traditional blood glucose monitoring methods. This system delivers real-time glucose values, trend arrows indicating the direction and rate of change, and alerts based on user-defined thresholds for high, low, or rapidly fluctuating glucose levels.

CGM is widely used in diabetes management to support informed decision-making regarding insulin dosing, dietary choices, and physical activity. It plays a critical role during illness, when glucose levels can become more volatile, by offering timely alerts and trend information. The data collected can be analyzed over time, with recommendations suggesting at least 14 consecutive days of use with 70% data coverage to optimize glycemic management. CGM systems also integrate with other diabetes technologies, such as insulin pumps, enabling features like automated insulin delivery and threshold-based suspension of insulin when glucose levels fall too low.

Despite its benefits, CGM has limitations. Sensor readings should not be used as the sole basis for treatment decisions, particularly during episodes of hypoglycemia, due to potential discrepancies between interstitial fluid glucose and blood glucose levels. Additionally, certain medications may interfere with sensor accuracy, and the system requires proper calibration and maintenance. CGM displays data directly on compatible devices, with glucose values and trend information visible on the home screen for easy access. Overall, Continuous Glucose Monitoring is a modern, valuable tool that enhances diabetes care by providing detailed insights into glycemic variability and supporting proactive management strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6828aacfba0c2a6ae59c439a4e4b161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bfad6c7b0e936c6a9c113798a2d963e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23235296c6ccfffc5ec397dd6b0dc7b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44b69a8e50a0a4230b6963fe643d8cd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03aab2b6261ddc73d474d0c866e7b37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6b7bb277d16feead8d082fc004d51d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2cf8a591a83e4436ab6a76c2d30e2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e910e1b2954d8b793bb4671174d8d92f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab4ddf6540d88f22cdfe4b96db42b88f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb234cb663874d4e5a473c7f3e19383b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77d9c07e6a38b29ecca31e8c42df37e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ef0e6e09cd17187cd967e6d49a3e718&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ad78a5db5d547aed7e2552fb1ec900&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc4a95eb5f47fc9afc3b30fb1364490&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04fa4143c79ef368662ed10679dc02f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d8c5703a9383d7fd4b6f1d14f5d5424&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60422265d298e0482f6b78dc36f2a16b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90de561bcfc1f3544a690722c75e6f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5904918561a819f1c95321943d91162&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-636a6ab7d0bef06e011ae7bf2140d2e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdbc37da05a342443ccfd48642dd1c3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9af645f149415b242f6b7443aeb536d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93fcb9633783dfd958b918dec57d02c8</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/monitoramento contínuo de glicose.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/glicemias capilares.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710140</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Continuous Glucose Monitoring</data>
</node>
<node id="Sensor">
  <data key="d0">Sensor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Sensor is a medical device used in continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) systems to measure glucose levels in real time, specifically in the interstitial fluid beneath the skin. It functions as a critical component of insulin pump systems, such as the MiniMed 780G, where it continuously monitors glucose levels and transmits data wirelessly to the insulin pump or compatible mobile devices every five minutes. The sensor must be properly inserted, paired, and calibrated—typically using blood glucose meter readings—to ensure accurate functionality and support advanced features like SmartGuard, which enables automated insulin delivery. Without an active sensor, the SmartGuard feature cannot operate, and users must rely on more frequent manual blood glucose checks.

The sensor integrates with a transmitter to relay glucose readings and is essential for real-time tracking of blood glucose levels. It requires a warm-up period after insertion and must be replaced upon expiration or if malfunctioning. Proper usage according to the user guide is necessary to avoid adverse reactions or inaccurate readings, particularly during the initial use phase. The device is sensitive to external conditions and must be protected from physical impact, strong magnetic fields, and radiofrequency (RF) interference, which can disrupt communication with the pump and trigger signal loss alerts. Exposure to MRI machines or other magnetic field-generating devices is strictly contraindicated.

Manufactured as part of CGM systems and designed by companies such as Abbott (used under license in certain Medtronic systems), the sensor plays a central role in diabetes management by enabling continuous, real-time glucose monitoring. Information regarding its calibration schedule, battery life, and operational status is typically accessible through the pump’s status menu. Overall, the sensor is a small but vital component that supports effective glucose control, automated insulin delivery, and timely alerts, contingent on correct use, maintenance, and environmental precautions.&lt;SEP&gt;A sensor is a small device applied to the body, such as the back of the arm, that measures interstitial glucose levels and transmits data to a reader or monitor in flash or continuous glucose monitoring systems.</data>
