  <data key="d5">1778709732</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Dexcom</data>
</node>
<node id="Calibration">
  <data key="d0">Calibration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Calibration is a required procedure for the sensor in the MiniMed™ 640G System, needed every 2 hours to maintain accurate glucose readings.&lt;SEP&gt;Calibration is the process of aligning the sensor glucose readings with blood glucose values using capillary blood glucose measurements.&lt;SEP&gt;Calibration is the process of adjusting a CGM system by inputting blood glucose readings to ensure accurate sensor glucose values.&lt;SEP&gt;Calibration is the process of entering a blood glucose value into the system to ensure accuracy of sensor glucose readings.&lt;SEP&gt;Calibration is the process by which the system aligns sensor glucose values with a manual BG meter reading to ensure accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Calibration is the process of aligning sensor glucose readings with blood glucose meter readings to improve accuracy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a08dc7008091e5ab01812c301e0aab1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46727635453cfb706e038b6920cb2d77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0f14c91b2fb52c8fcc6d43aa4f53f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03aab2b6261ddc73d474d0c866e7b37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86d3bb81c7595510175054c288b122fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecb2783153f277b98dbb01bd056cf9a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63f9bd562c68fc510780d6f6629360a4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Calibration</data>
</node>
<node id="Children">
  <data key="d0">Children</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Children are individuals typically defined within the age range of 2 to 17 years in medical and clinical contexts, with specific subgroups such as those aged 1–6 years or 6–12 years referenced in pharmacokinetic studies. They are recognized as a vulnerable patient group requiring tailored approaches to medication administration, particularly for insulin therapy. Due to their thinner skin and limited subcutaneous tissue, children require specific injection techniques, including the use of shorter needles (such as 4 mm) and a 45° needle insertion angle, especially when using needles 6 mm or longer. In some guidelines, a subcutaneous pinch combined with a 45° angle is recommended for all needle lengths to ensure proper subcutaneous delivery and avoid intramuscular injection.

Precise insulin dosing is critical for children, who often rely on specialized equipment to ensure safe and accurate administration. Medications, including insulin products such as Novolin® R and Novolin® R Penfill®, must be stored securely out of children's reach to prevent accidental ingestion, misuse, or unintended administration. This precaution extends to sharps containers, which must also be inaccessible to children due to the risk of injury from used needles. Additionally, children are at risk of choking if they place small parts of medical devices or packaging in their mouths, highlighting broader safety concerns in the design and handling of pharmaceutical products.

Clinical studies have included children across various age groups to evaluate drug safety and pharmacokinetics. For instance, Levemir has been studied in children aged 1–17 years, with findings indicating no significant differences in pharmacokinetic profiles compared to adults. Children above 2 years of age have also been included in long-term studies assessing antibody formation during Levemir treatment. Similarly, pharmacokinetic data on Novolin® R have been collected from children aged 6 to 12 years, with limited evidence suggesting similarity to adult insulin profiles. These findings support the inclusion of pediatric populations in diabetes management strategies while emphasizing the need for age-appropriate administration practices and vigilant safety measures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191286ebd759321d40620f77c84ef5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fee4c8681a7629eb6ff0eb2470a98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bbc7b2df438b8290a4da57f63f1ff5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb4a4dec39574c303988e93b87c351d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a4232687a569d10d6942d933172488f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d59277cc4b25262a26a88992bfb526f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1d7f92cdb7923807a9067d937837a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001d95b4722fb6304af5e929d80557ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a0b8b861ae9af417a572907fe16408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4f0986d34b7b73e98fe14c73abfb6f3</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710173</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Children</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Type 1">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Type 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes Type 1 is a condition where the body does not produce insulin at all, requiring daily insulin use.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 1 is a medical condition in which individuals are more likely to develop lipo-hypertrophy due to long-term insulin use.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes Type 1 is an autoimmune condition where the body does not produce insulin, commonly diagnosed in children and adolescents, requiring lifelong insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes type 1 is a condition where patients typically require 0.5 to 1.0 UI/kg/day of insulin, with higher needs in pre-adolescents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a8e3b3c250bb6776d69bf991a7c68dcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-525086e26636af026c417773a8f11a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1d7f92cdb7923807a9067d937837a4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709975</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Diabetes Type 1</data>
</node>
<node id="REPLACE-BG Study">
  <data key="d0">REPLACE-BG Study</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The REPLACE-BG study demonstrated that using CGM data directly for insulin dosing is safe and effective in well-controlled adults with type 1 diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6828aacfba0c2a6ae59c439a4e4b161</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1778708986</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">REPLACE-BG Study</data>
</node>
<node id="Glucometer">
  <data key="d0">Glucometer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A device used to measure blood glucose levels by analyzing a blood sample applied to a test strip.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucometer is a device used to measure capillary blood glucose levels, requiring a blood sample from the fingertip.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7d99aa72407a6721b69e4da3cbbcba7</data>
