<node id="Subcutaneous Tissue">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous Tissue</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Subcutaneous tissue is the layer of body fat located beneath the skin, serving as the primary site for insulin administration in individuals managing diabetes. This tissue layer is critical for the delivery of insulin, whether through injections or insulin pumps, as it allows for the gradual absorption of the hormone into the bloodstream. The thickness of subcutaneous tissue varies depending on age, body location, and individual characteristics, and its health is essential for predictable and effective insulin absorption.

Insulin must be injected directly into healthy subcutaneous tissue to ensure proper delivery and to minimize the risk of complications. Areas affected by conditions such as lipodystrophy or inflammation should be avoided, as compromised tissue can lead to erratic insulin uptake and reduced glycemic control. Proper injection technique involves targeting intact, well-vascularized fat layers to promote consistent pharmacokinetics.

When insulin is delivered via subcutaneous routes, it must travel through this fatty layer to reach blood vessels and target cells, resulting in a physiological delay in insulin action. This inherent lag is an important consideration in diabetes management, influencing dosing timing and glucose monitoring strategies. Overall, the integrity and proper use of subcutaneous tissue are vital for safe and effective insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Subcutaneous tissue is the body layer where insulin is infused via the catheter from the pump.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5ea5432a4b376d5a48f31d6694c76d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e490a7e963a20e2221a8097b50073543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b0f8a5b356409be05a1e866ce0ed8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78692c868b2064d1fbeaf7aec1c44948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb4a4dec39574c303988e93b87c351d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-180dc3d244f7d832cf505b09cfc34746&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4082de8021392199c251dff122fdc80b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbbd13d3e688c25e68d838e8069fec5c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/CGM/monitoramento contínuo de glicose.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Subcutaneous Tissue</data>
</node>
<node id="Needle Length">
  <data key="d0">Needle Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Needle length refers to the size of insulin injection needles, which range from 4 to 12 mm, with specific recommendations based on patient age, BMI, and tissue availability.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle length is a factor in insulin injection recommendations, influenced by skin and subcutaneous adipose layer thickness in diabetic patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle length is a critical factor in insulin injection, with shorter needles (4–6 mm) recommended to minimize the risk of intramuscular injection.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle length is a factor in insulin injection that influences the required angle of insertion and the need for a subcutaneous pinch, with common lengths being 4 mm, 5 mm, 6 mm, and 8 mm.&lt;SEP&gt;Needle length varies in insulin syringes, with options of 6, 8, and 12.7 mm, where 6 mm is considered the best option for injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae9010ed1abd5a5160b82713c862d22f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee3024e95a663f89ec3c7a35028cc636&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fc83e266bdeb54c4139a50adf2b793a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea5432a4b376d5a48f31d6694c76d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bbc7b2df438b8290a4da57f63f1ff5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c66bff09b476d15a54c71ff3efac594d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/transporte e conservação Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-20201.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709119</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Needle Length</data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Glucose Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Blood Glucose Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood Glucose Monitoring is the process of regularly measuring blood sugar levels to manage diabetes effectively and prevent acute and long-term complications such as hypoglycemia, diabetic ketoacidosis, and progressive diabetic complications. This monitoring is typically performed using a glucometer to check capillary blood glucose levels or through continuous glucose sensors, with confirmation via blood testing before making critical therapy decisions. It is an essential component of diabetes care, particularly for individuals on intensive insulin therapy, insulin pumps, or Novolin® R infusion, where frequent adjustments to insulin dosing are required to maintain glycemic control.

The practice involves multiple daily measurements, often recommended before meals, at bedtime, before driving, during air travel—when pressure changes may affect insulin delivery—and in response to changes in insulin injection sites. Shifting injection sites, especially from affected to healthy tissue, can introduce glycemic variability, necessitating intensified monitoring to ensure stable blood glucose levels. Regular and consistent monitoring helps optimize glycemic control, guide insulin administration, and support timely therapeutic interventions.

Blood Glucose Monitoring is especially critical during pregnancy, when tight glycemic control is vital for maternal and fetal health, and in dynamic clinical situations requiring close observation. By enabling early detection of fluctuations, it plays a key role in preventing both hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia, supporting safe daily activities, and ultimately delaying the onset of diabetes-related complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d4cfc50689ef0b918230df31cb99e5e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea5432a4b376d5a48f31d6694c76d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b2243846b349d5b78af57a8e77b6ff5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11ac81b4dee4dbb3636b8dc2403e0661&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da213aa66bddbd8e1bd2aba01ceb9f2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1d7f92cdb7923807a9067d937837a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9fa1ee045093083e543d672f1309db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2339435aa42eeb6f0cb8bffbf4d5184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23235296c6ccfffc5ec397dd6b0dc7b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f63b580c3efbe7e9a671badd7807ad11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44b69a8e50a0a4230b6963fe643d8cd0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6367cc87b3326e358622e179b72cbfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3000f8880af4ef448483b353db654f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26f5ebaa0341f051ad33cfb9895a3851&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395e34987ebd21d82a5ef4f460731296&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35705afcd32698e0eb1756f2c3484a4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Cópia de Técnicas de aplicação de insulina.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Armazenamento e Transporte/Cuidados com a Insulinoterapia conservar.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710155</data>
