  <data key="d5">1778709101</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Insulin Pens</data>
</node>
<node id="Hiperglicemia">
  <data key="d0">Hiperglicemia</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Hiperglicemia refers to high blood glucose levels, a condition commonly associated with diabetes mellitus, particularly when blood sugar exceeds 10 mmol/L. It is characterized by symptoms such as excessive thirst, fatigue, and, if left untreated, can progress to serious complications like diabetic ketoacidosis, especially in individuals with type 1 diabetes. The condition arises from a variety of factors that disrupt effective glucose regulation, primarily involving insufficient insulin activity.

Multiple causes contribute to the development of hiperglicemia. These include inadequate insulin dosing, discontinuation of insulin treatment, improper insulin absorption due to repeated injections in areas with lipohypertrophy, lipoatrophy, or localized cutaneous amyloidosis, and incorrect insulin administration techniques. Additionally, external factors such as overeating, illness, and improper handling of insulin delivery devices—such as insulin pens leading to leakage—can also result in elevated blood glucose levels. In hospitalized patients, particularly those in intensive care, hiperglicemia is a critical concern requiring prompt management.

Treatment strategies for hiperglicemia often involve the use of short-acting insulin formulations such as Novolin® R, especially in acute or intensive care settings. Clinical studies have focused on managing blood glucose levels above 10 mmol/L with this intervention, emphasizing precise insulin application and monitoring to ensure effective absorption and glycemic control. Proper injection site rotation and patient education on insulin handling are essential to prevent recurrence and complications associated with chronic hyperglycemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2dff1c37dd504c2e0f6d3e3541019e15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51960dcc8f6bd8e9d62d3512fa1e35af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c96e22adae6d8c02ec9ce8430d0cf64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba8a91b916675f6f4ef560f6976957e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e53353cd9fcafe8d9581b3d0e7980b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a0f540a03b366d4d206f9c895849de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1d9cc89371adea01fc1a946ca6d1690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-581b6c5da621e9091266d2b0e1919308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19de4016c46aba524b40e489fd60f018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-619da52df301ce6db70795213fac37be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5674b4a4db2a056560132addcc354</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710066</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Hiperglicemia</data>
</node>
