  <data key="d7">Diabetes Mellitus</data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin resistance is a condition in which the body's cells do not respond effectively to insulin, commonly associated with type 2 diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin Resistance is a condition where the body's cells do not respond effectively to insulin, increasing daily insulin requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin resistance, such as during puberty or due to obesity, increases daily insulin requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin resistance increases daily insulin requirements, such as during puberty or due to obesity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d59277cc4b25262a26a88992bfb526f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1d7f92cdb7923807a9067d937837a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9fa1ee045093083e543d672f1309db</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710014</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Insulin Resistance</data>
</node>
<node id="Pancreas">
  <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
  <data key="d1">organ</data>
  <data key="d2">The pancreas is an organ in the human body that plays a critical role in regulating blood glucose levels through the production and release of key hormones, primarily insulin and glucagon. It functions as both an endocrine and exocrine gland, but its hormonal role is central to glucose homeostasis. In non-diabetic individuals, the pancreas naturally produces insulin continuously, modulating its output in response to blood glucose concentrations to maintain metabolic balance.

Insulin, a hormone essential for facilitating glucose uptake into cells, is synthesized and secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas. The organ produces two types of insulin secretion: basal insulin, which provides a steady background level to manage glucose between meals and during fasting, and bolus insulin, which is released in response to meals to handle spikes in blood sugar. This dynamic regulation ensures that glucose levels remain within a normal physiological range. In addition to insulin, the pancreas produces glucagon, a hormone that acts in opposition to insulin by stimulating the liver to release stored glucose, thereby preventing hypoglycemia.

In type 1 diabetes, the pancreas is unable to produce sufficient insulin because the body's immune system attacks and destroys the insulin-producing beta cells. As a result, individuals with this condition require external insulin administration. Medical technologies such as insulin pump therapy are designed to mimic the natural insulin delivery pattern of a healthy pancreas, attempting to replicate both basal and bolus insulin secretion. Overall, the pancreas is indispensable for metabolic regulation, and its proper function is vital for preventing disorders related to glucose imbalance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2a0a3f492aa5ef7771b2246547cb5f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd7b4fa0ccd15d540a9299e77dbf1dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cd2f791e7cc88d9bd36c809feb7b626&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d2c98e899214ad3a2eb3b7863c5d5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6900fc7e5ef921c29efe412333f2e7ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20fd2bf061c46e98e4d7791fe6728699&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fbdbcaae1740f3543ed8a673ff203b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b139e060742a21ffc13eee0fdbf0be4a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709396</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Pancreas</data>
</node>
<node id="Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose is the primary source of energy for the body, derived from carbohydrates in food, transported via the bloodstream, and used by cells with the help of insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a sugar that serves as a primary source of energy for cells and enters cells with the help of insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a sugar that serves as a primary energy source in the body and is monitored in diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a natural substance in the blood that serves as a primary energy source for the body and is monitored closely in diabetes management.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a simple sugar that serves as a primary energy source in the body and is critical to monitor in individuals with insulin therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a simple carbohydrate used by the body as its primary energy source, essential for functions such as heartbeat, breathing, and brain activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Glucose is a primary source of cellular energy that must enter cells via insulin; its levels are regulated by insulin and glucagon.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4cd2f791e7cc88d9bd36c809feb7b626&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d2c98e899214ad3a2eb3b7863c5d5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c03aab2b6261ddc73d474d0c866e7b37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa5dc0d158b3af5c0f7883f98cce6d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3000f8880af4ef448483b353db654f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7fbdbcaae1740f3543ed8a673ff203b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b139e060742a21ffc13eee0fdbf0be4a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710163</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Glucose</data>
</node>
<node id="Retinopathy">
  <data key="d0">Retinopathy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Retinopathy is a diabetes complication affecting the eyes, caused by prolonged high blood sugar levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f731774e5187eb76527ebeabefeeeb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b91b40e47c97be1b62c0f3d271cd4efd</data>
