  <data key="d7">Blood Glucose Level</data>
</node>
<node id="Infection">
  <data key="d0">Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infection is a medical condition that may lead to increased ketone levels and requires monitoring for diabetic ketoacidosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Infection is a physiological stressor that can elevate blood glucose and ketone levels, necessitating ketone monitoring in diabetic individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Infection is a potential complication from using non-sterile or reused needles during insulin injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ea83f440d1d3472e7a19b4ca0d941320&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d212ccdded1e4ccf51af243a8d38b4c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778709907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Infection</data>
</node>
<node id="Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Pregnancy is a physiological state characterized by significant metabolic and hormonal changes that directly impact glucose metabolism and insulin requirements, particularly in women with pre-existing or gestational diabetes. During pregnancy, insulin needs typically fluctuate across trimesters, necessitating intensified glycemic monitoring and frequent dose adjustments to maintain optimal blood glucose control and prevent maternal and fetal complications. This condition requires careful interpretation of diagnostic tools such as the HbA1c test, as physiological changes during pregnancy can affect its accuracy, making continuous glucose monitoring and additional clinical assessments essential.

Insulin therapy is permitted, recommended, and considered safe during pregnancy because insulin does not cross the placental barrier, thereby avoiding direct fetal exposure. However, administration techniques must be adapted to ensure effective delivery and patient comfort. In early pregnancy, standard injection practices apply, but as pregnancy progresses into the third trimester, abdominal insulin injections are generally avoided due to anatomical and safety concerns. Instead, alternative injection sites are used, and specific techniques—such as employing a 4 mm needle at a 45° angle—are recommended to ensure subcutaneous delivery and minimize complications.

The use of specific insulin formulations, such as Levemir (detemir), has been studied extensively in pregnant women with diabetes. Research has evaluated Levemir’s safety and efficacy in comparison to other basal insulins, particularly in those with pre-existing diabetes. These studies support the use of Levemir during pregnancy under medical supervision, indicating it is an acceptable option for managing basal insulin needs. Overall, pregnancy demands a tailored, dynamic approach to insulin therapy, integrating safe practices, appropriate technology, and close medical oversight to optimize outcomes for both mother and child.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457b20f4d8d08ee6b3ed08a225c773de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e490a7e963a20e2221a8097b50073543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b0f8a5b356409be05a1e866ce0ed8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da213aa66bddbd8e1bd2aba01ceb9f2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00bac63e27caeb094fe802c0bfe00a44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9266b0855586c9a971861beb4d3289fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ee5ffd422bf5c38703d11eefbcff6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc71d03de36b3a734f3f7eff9922da5d</data>
