<node id="Bomba De Insulina">
  <data key="d0">Bomba De Insulina</data>
  <data key="d1">equipment</data>
  <data key="d2">Bomba De Insulina is a medical device designed to deliver insulin in a controlled and continuous manner, serving as an external replacement for the natural insulin production of a healthy pancreas, particularly in individuals with diabetes, including those with type 1 diabetes. The device administers rapid-acting insulin in small, precise amounts throughout the day and night to meet basal insulin needs, while also allowing for bolus doses to cover carbohydrate intake or correct high blood glucose levels. This dual functionality—supporting both basal and bolus insulin delivery—enables more accurate glycemic control and mimics the physiological insulin secretion of a functioning pancreas.

The Bomba De Insulina operates by delivering insulin every few minutes through an infusion set connected to the body, ensuring a steady and adjustable flow tailored to the patient’s metabolic requirements. Advanced features such as dual and square wave boluses allow for customized insulin delivery, accommodating complex meal patterns or delayed digestion. The device is especially critical for people with type 1 diabetes, as any interruption in insulin delivery can rapidly lead to diabetic ketoacidosis, a life-threatening condition. Consequently, reliable operation is essential, and failure of the system poses significant health risks.

Some models of the Bomba De Insulina are integrated into advanced diabetes management systems, such as the MiniMed™ 640G, and may be combined with continuous glucose monitoring technologies like Enlite to enhance safety and efficacy. These integrated systems can automatically adjust insulin delivery and include features designed to reduce the risk of hypoglycemia. Overall, the Bomba De Insulina provides a flexible, precise, and physiologically responsive method of insulin therapy, improving quality of life and glycemic outcomes for individuals dependent on exogenous insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c0ec5707b6df65f1a6822e01b9bb3fb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6b0367ed2176146798e5cf697d0aeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39f2ee843ae28cc44b37c7f51f7722a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59696e5e7f94ff9d51e39d90ec169e17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-357e07e3b068c4c6d31ac6913e35178f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6900fc7e5ef921c29efe412333f2e7ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7c10f0a011d51190fa761b5c0aff4ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0fdf7fe6f512616144e110952e56f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01ce95c3ebe034549347b078008daa3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17d4cfa9183f9c1eb5a0c2212479127a</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Bomba De Insulina</data>
</node>
<node id="CGM">
  <data key="d0">CGM</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CGM, or Continuous Glucose Monitoring, is a technology designed for the continuous tracking of glucose levels in individuals with diabetes. It operates through a system that includes a small sensor inserted beneath the skin, typically placed in the interstitial fluid of the abdominal or subcutaneous tissue, which measures glucose levels in real time. This sensor is often connected to or works in conjunction with a transmitter that sends data to a display device, such as a dedicated receiver, insulin pump, or smartphone application, enabling users and healthcare providers to monitor glucose trends and fluctuations throughout the day and night.

The primary function of CGM is to provide ongoing, real-time glucose readings, reducing the need for frequent fingerstick blood glucose tests. By continuously tracking glucose levels, CGM systems help users identify patterns, detect hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia early, and make informed decisions about insulin dosing, diet, and physical activity. The technology is particularly beneficial for individuals with type 1 diabetes, though it is also used by some with type 2 diabetes who require insulin therapy. Various CGM systems integrate with insulin pumps, such as those using a Guardian transmitter, to support automated insulin delivery in closed-loop systems. Overall, CGM enhances diabetes management by offering a comprehensive, dynamic view of glucose control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6828aacfba0c2a6ae59c439a4e4b161&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bcf8f1ff0075b1e2f1edaa4bc946a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ef4fd0fa0c26a81460d7687cc37cf95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1dfc786d81b6125f9012b67e900b5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2667d6936a8a9a26e886ba90b060796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8080e2e312ed6994d0dc059a4aeca5f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c79aff2bfb4c32e8089ffd4b1e5eb5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85d195e1f8c488146a71cdd978e8c82</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">CGM</data>
</node>
<node id="Capillary Blood Glucose">
  <data key="d0">Capillary Blood Glucose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Capillary blood glucose is measured via finger prick and provides real-time glucose readings, used for immediate decision-making such as insulin dosing or treating hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Capillary blood glucose is a measurement used to confirm episodes of hypoglycemia, defined as levels below 2.8 mmol/L or 3.1 mmol/L (50.4 mg/dL or 55.8 mg/dL) when expressed as plasma glucose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975d0ad130a42cf2fe8a0f91e4da2b29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a1c7be3f5278cc80c8bc958c35be31c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710100</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Capillary Blood Glucose</data>
</node>
<node id="Time in Range">
  <data key="d0">Time in Range</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Time in Range (TIR) is a metric that shows the percentage of time a person’s blood glucose remains between 70 and 180 mg/dL, ideally above 70% of the time.&lt;SEP&gt;Time in Range is the percentage of time sensor glucose levels are between 70 mg/dL and 180 mg/dL, a fixed range that cannot be modified.&lt;SEP&gt;Time in range refers to the percentage of time a person's glucose levels remain within a target range, a key metric in modern glycemic control.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b3a357a9c2bacf7aeb20d6f0357d7dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a1c7be3f5278cc80c8bc958c35be31c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/GLICEMIAS CAPILARES/glicemias capilares.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710146</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Time in Range</data>
</node>
<node id="Healthy Elderly">
  <data key="d0">Healthy Elderly</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Healthy elderly individuals (65+ years) with diabetes may aim for HbA1c up to 7.5% and fasting glucose of 80–130 mg/dL, depending on overall health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710096</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Healthy Elderly</data>
</node>
<node id="Severely Compromised Elderly">
  <data key="d0">Severely Compromised Elderly</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Severely compromised elderly individuals, such as bedridden patients with serious illnesses, focus on avoiding extreme glucose levels without strict numerical targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-986fc7ecc813c4b7942964d7fd64d429</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710053</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Severely Compromised Elderly</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Descompensado">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Descompensado</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bcba435392a61e072dfaad9195e42ed7</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetonas are produced in conditions like Diabetes Descompensado when the body lacks sufficient glucose for energy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetonas are produced when the body lacks sufficient glucose, such as in uncontrolled diabetes, leading to fat burning for energy.</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/MONITORIZAÇÃO GLICÊMICA/METAS GLICÊMICAS/Documento sem título.docx</data>
  <data key="d5">1778710052</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Diabetes Descompensado</data>
</node>
