</node>
<node id="Correction Bolus">
  <data key="d0">Correction Bolus</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Correction Bolus is an insulin dose administered to lower blood glucose levels that are above the target range, with the goal of bringing them back to a desired target level. It is commonly used in diabetes management to address hyperglycemia and is often calculated using specific parameters such as the current blood glucose level, the target glucose level, the individual’s insulin sensitivity factor (ISF), and the amount of active insulin still remaining in the body. This calculation ensures a precise and safe insulin adjustment to avoid hypoglycemia.

The Correction Bolus can be determined manually or through automated systems such as the Bolus Wizard feature available on some insulin pumps, which integrates the necessary variables to recommend an appropriate dose. It is typically based on blood glucose meter readings, especially in situations where substances like acetaminophen or paracetamol are present, as these can interfere with continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) sensor accuracy and thus affect dosing decisions. The administration of a Correction Bolus is often displayed on device interfaces and may be visualized on sensor glucose graphs to track its impact over time.

While most descriptions emphasize the use of a Correction Bolus for high blood glucose or sensor glucose (BG/SG) readings, one source notes its use in adjusting levels that are either above or below the target range, which may reflect a broader interpretation or potential confusion with other types of bolus doses. However, the overwhelming consensus defines the Correction Bolus specifically for hyperglycemic correction. In all cases, the primary function remains the reduction of elevated glucose levels to a predetermined target using a calculated insulin dose.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec9910e322fb247c0bf85c8b8cb3e948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95f095f73e1ec982aa57fd66798ffaec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d0f18d3b5098557dfd71a1955adc3a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0f14c91b2fb52c8fcc6d43aa4f53f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfb7ffc723b939ad50f1b00ca6ec8b9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-425759c12d133ef4832928e8f5d50896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb3ef3e959b16d99ac4debc0210965b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac9a59cce7c2f6a229062408b8278675&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe3d4956e4290b26b4d22092a403c4d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8080e2e312ed6994d0dc059a4aeca5f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cc4a95eb5f47fc9afc3b30fb1364490&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90de561bcfc1f3544a690722c75e6f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5904918561a819f1c95321943d91162</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
