<node id="Insulina NPH">
  <data key="d0">Insulina NPH</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina NPH, also known as neutral protamine Hagedorn insulin, is an intermediate-acting insulin formulation used in the management of diabetes mellitus. It functions as a basal insulin, providing glucose control over an extended period, with an onset of action within 1 to 2 hours after administration, a peak effect occurring between 4 and 12 hours, and a duration of activity lasting up to 18 to 24 hours. The medication has a milky appearance due to its suspension form and must be homogenized—gently rolled or mixed—before use to ensure consistent dosing; failure to do so can result in erratic absorption and increase the risk of hypoglycemia.

Insulina NPH is administered via subcutaneous injection, commonly using reusable insulin pens, and is available in 3 mL vials provided by the Brazilian public health system (SUS) for this purpose. It can be combined in the same syringe with regular or ultra-rapid-acting insulins, although when mixed, it should be drawn after the regular insulin to prevent contamination. This flexibility in combination therapy makes it a practical option in both type 1 and type 2 diabetes treatment regimens.

In clinical research, Insulina NPH has been frequently used as a comparator, particularly against newer basal insulins such as insulin detemir (Levemir®). Studies, including those involving pregnant women with type 1 diabetes, have shown that Insulina NPH is associated with higher variability in absorption and action, a greater risk of nocturnal hypoglycemia, and increased weight gain compared to insulin detemir. Despite these limitations, it remains a widely used and accessible option in diabetes care, especially in resource-limited settings, due to its established efficacy and lower cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4654b5e9fa02cf051c2d5ebc846c5d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-022aabbd0c94aa681dcd00c49d072b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60fe44346c0b02882f7625cbf43d8ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de80027ca96b7501497bcb85811b669d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c897fc79a7dde2715e95be7ca23f57f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c96e22adae6d8c02ec9ce8430d0cf64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc624fe52553cbd90a82f4181c5c0be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80de93eae49980367cf11700b83b1ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ced7d025f7f3232d1a0e28251b0fb63a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cba8a91b916675f6f4ef560f6976957e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24500f5f12a941115e4e44763af8133&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/DOC Completo.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Guia-Pratico-SBD-Canetas-Reutilizaveis-de-Insulina-no-SUS.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/insulinas em seringa.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/uso da insulina - Diretrizes-Sociedade-Brasileira-de-Diabetes-2019-2020.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698077</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
  <data key="d7">Insulina NPH</data>
</node>
