</node>
<node id="Dinamarca">
  <data key="d0">Dinamarca</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Dinamarca is the country where Novo Nordisk A/S, the owner of the registered trademarks Levemir®, FlexPen®, and NovoFine®, is located.&lt;SEP&gt;Dinamarca is the country where Novo Nordisk A/S, the owner of the Novolin® and FlexPen® trademarks, is located.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bad7b7e8e5212b6d7ea0f4b0505f82c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b93b5769e8df2e4aa6c2c1dc9e65fd85</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698963</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ganho De Peso">
  <data key="d0">Ganho De Peso</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ganho De Peso refers to weight increase associated with insulin therapy, which was lower with Levemir than with NPH insulin in clinical trials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698110</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Estudo Combinado Liraglutida+Metformina E Levemir">
  <data key="d0">Estudo Combinado Liraglutida+Metformina E Levemir</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Estudo Combinado Liraglutida+Metformina E Levemir is a randomized open-label trial evaluating the addition of Levemir to liraglutida and metformina in type 2 diabetes patients not reaching glycemic targets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698110</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperglycemic Episodes With Ketosis">
  <data key="d0">Hyperglycemic Episodes With Ketosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperglycemic Episodes With Ketosis are acute complications in diabetes, observed at a significantly higher rate with Levemir compared to insulin degludeca in a clinical study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698110</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adolescent Patients">
  <data key="d0">Adolescent Patients</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Adolescent Patients are a patient group for whom Levemir is indicated for type 2 diabetes, based on extrapolated efficacy and safety data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-302e8ebf2cacce3164fd241fd39db173</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698111</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Difference">
  <data key="d0">Risk Difference</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e7e33f93b79c1061267614c8283508c6</data>
  <data key="d2">The adjusted risk difference (95% CI) was calculated using propensity score matching to account for baseline differences between treatment groups.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698210</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Human">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Human</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a4232687a569d10d6942d933172488f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff021af578e58080f6a865c5f105e6d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43208e8857e43453277beecb7a6c55d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2e44a4ca961f1a97305607fa263ed91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-128e097e2326548df575159ee000923b</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin detemir has reduced affinity for insulin and IGF-1 receptors and reduced cell growth effects compared to human insulin.&gt;&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin human is the active substance in Novolin® R, degraded by insulin protease or insulin-degrading enzymes, with a terminal half-life of about 2–5 hours determined by absorption rate from subcutaneous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin human is a hormone used in diabetes treatment, present in Novolin® R at concentrations between 0.05 UI/mL and 1.0 UI/mL in infusion fluids.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin human is a hormone used in diabetes treatment, delivered via Novolin® R FlexPen®, and administered in concentrations from 0.05 UI/mL to 1.0 UI/mL for intravenous infusion.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin human is a medication used in diabetes management, and its dosage requirements can be affected by various medical conditions, medications, and lifestyle factors.</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778699592</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Requirement">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Requirement</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a4232687a569d10d6942d933172488f</data>
  <data key="d2">Concomitant illnesses such as infections increase patient insulin requirements and may necessitate dose adjustments.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698231</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intoxicação">
  <data key="d0">Intoxicação</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6ca90008a805c9c66eac7ba65db90fdb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d717433722cc9ac8e746440493df9010</data>
  <data key="d2">In case of intoxication, individuals are advised to call 0800 722 6001 for immediate assistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Intoxicação refers to poisoning or harmful reaction due to medication, with a dedicated hotline for guidance.</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778699605</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absorption">
  <data key="d0">Absorption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Absorption of insulin occurs subcutaneously, with peak plasma concentration reached within 1.5 to 2.5 hours, and terminal half-life dependent on absorption rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Absorption is the process by which insulin enters the bloodstream, reaching peak plasma concentration within 1.5 to 2.5 hours post-administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin shows no strong binding to plasma proteins except circulating insulin antibodies when present.&lt;SEP&gt;Distribution of insulin in the body shows minimal binding to plasma proteins except circulating insulin antibodies when present.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metabolism">
  <data key="d0">Metabolism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin is degraded by insulin protease, insulin-degrading enzymes, and possibly protein disulfide isomerase, with proposed cleavage sites and inactive metabolites.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolism of human insulin involves degradation by insulin protease, insulin-degrading enzymes, and possibly protein disulfide isomerase, with inactive metabolites formed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43208e8857e43453277beecb7a6c55d5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elimination">
  <data key="d0">Elimination</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The terminal half-life of insulin is determined by subcutaneous absorption rate, not by direct elimination from plasma, where insulin has a half-life of a few minutes.&lt;SEP&gt;Elimination of insulin is characterized by a terminal half-life of 2 to 5 hours, determined by absorption rate from subcutaneous tissue.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Novolin® R Penfill®">
  <data key="d0">Novolin® R Penfill®</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Novolin® R Penfill® is an insulin formulation that must not be used in continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion pumps due to risk of catheter precipitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Novolin® R Penfill® is an insulin product presented in a cartridge (carpule) format for use with insulin pens, requiring specific storage and handling to maintain stability and prevent medication errors.&lt;SEP&gt;Novolin® R Penfill® is a brand-name formulation of fast-acting insulin, presented as a clear, colorless, aqueous solution for subcutaneous or intravenous use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a0b8b861ae9af417a572907fe16408</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypersensitivity">
  <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypersensitivity is a contraindication for Novolin® R, occurring in patients with known allergy to the active substance or any excipients in the formulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients is a contraindication for the use of Novolin® R.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients is a contraindication for the use of insulin products.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypersensitivity to the active substance or excipients is a contraindication for using Novolin® R FlexPen®.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001d95b4722fb6304af5e929d80557ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43208e8857e43453277beecb7a6c55d5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Zone Travel">
  <data key="d0">Time Zone Travel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Travel across time zones requires medical consultation as it may necessitate changes in insulin administration and meal timing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698935</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Transfer">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Transfer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Switching between types, brands, or origins of insulin must be done under strict medical supervision due to potential dose adjustments and differences in warning symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Transferring patients from other insulins to Novolin® R FlexPen® requires strict medical supervision due to potential dose and regimen changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698936</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terminal Half-Life">
  <data key="d0">Terminal Half-Life</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Terminal half-life (t ½) refers to the time it takes for insulin to be absorbed from subcutaneous tissue, not eliminated from plasma, estimated at 2–5 hours.&lt;SEP&gt;The terminal half-life (t½) of insulin is approximately 2 to 5 hours, reflecting absorption rate from subcutaneous tissue rather than plasma clearance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001d95b4722fb6304af5e929d80557ac</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698937</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Novolin® R">
  <data key="d0">Novolin® R</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Novolin® R is a brand-name pharmaceutical product containing recombinant human insulin, specifically formulated as a fast-acting or short-acting insulin for the treatment of diabetes mellitus. It is available as a clear, colorless aqueous solution at a concentration of 100 UI/mL and is provided in multiple presentations, including 10 mL vials, cartridges, and pre-filled delivery devices such as the FlexPen®. This insulin formulation is indicated for glycemic control in both adult and pediatric populations, with pharmacokinetic properties studied across age groups.

Novolin® R is administered via subcutaneous, intravenous, or intramuscular injection, with onset of action typically occurring within half an hour, a peak effect between 1.5 and 3.5 hours, and a duration of action lasting approximately 7 to 8 hours. The pharmacokinetic profile may vary based on factors such as injection site, dosage, and individual patient characteristics. It is particularly used for mealtime insulin coverage due to its rapid onset.

The medication requires specific storage, handling, and administration protocols to maintain efficacy and prevent complications. Importantly, Novolin® R must not be used in continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII) pumps, including insulin pump systems, due to the risk of precipitation and subsequent delivery failure. It is suitable for intravenous infusion in clinical settings but is primarily administered subcutaneously in routine diabetes management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df59f6460ce09b02f1576e31831b6eb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e53353cd9fcafe8d9581b3d0e7980b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d59277cc4b25262a26a88992bfb526f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff021af578e58080f6a865c5f105e6d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54b65b433ba47e8bbf2d607b89776246&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e1d7f92cdb7923807a9067d937837a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4db353458c93e1b1abc9f696ebf195a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82a0f540a03b366d4d206f9c895849de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1d9cc89371adea01fc1a946ca6d1690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001d95b4722fb6304af5e929d80557ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc3409d91fe33592f741a0002d70749c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2339435aa42eeb6f0cb8bffbf4d5184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-581b6c5da621e9091266d2b0e1919308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43208e8857e43453277beecb7a6c55d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbd230d2ccb1901e088f3bb5714424fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ae5fe50adf25db0be3b8cdde6282e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc71d03de36b3a734f3f7eff9922da5d</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698941</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driving">
  <data key="d0">Driving</data>
  <data key="d1">activity</data>
  <data key="d2">Driving is an activity that may be impaired due to hypoglycemia in diabetic patients, requiring precautionary measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Driving is an activity that may be impaired due to hypoglycemia, requiring patients to take precautions if they experience reduced or absent hypoglycemia warning signs.&lt;SEP&gt;Driving is an activity that may be impaired due to hypoglycemia, requiring patients to take precautions, especially if they have reduced hypoglycemia awareness or frequent episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Driving is an activity that requires safe blood glucose levels above the target threshold to ensure the safety of the individual and others on the road.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ee5ffd422bf5c38703d11eefbcff6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc71d03de36b3a734f3f7eff9922da5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3000f8880af4ef448483b353db654f9</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778702595</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medication Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Medication Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Medication interactions refer to the effects of various drugs on insulin requirements, either increasing or decreasing the need for insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Medication interactions refer to the effects of various drugs on insulin requirements, either increasing or decreasing them, and potentially masking hypoglycemia symptoms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ee5ffd422bf5c38703d11eefbcff6f</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698947</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors">
  <data key="d0">Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monoamine oxidase inhibitors are drugs that can reduce insulin requirements and increase the risk of hypoglycemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Monoamine oxidase inhibitors are medications that can reduce a patient's insulin requirements and may interact with glucose metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO inhibitors) can reduce insulin requirements in patients using insulin therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc71d03de36b3a734f3f7eff9922da5d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-128e097e2326548df575159ee000923b</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778699596</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Travel">
  <data key="d0">Travel</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Travel across time zones may require adjustments in insulin administration and meal timing, necessitating medical consultation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2df8f5e1333eed2f8c4d2dca734d5e21</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698954</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Therapy Intensification">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Therapy Intensification</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin therapy intensification may alter hypoglycemia warning symptoms, requiring patient education and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensification of insulin therapy can lead to significantly improved glycemic control in patients with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e5674b4a4db2a056560132addcc354</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778699613</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cetone Breath">
  <data key="d0">Cetone Breath</data>
  <data key="d1">symptom</data>
  <data key="d2">Cetone breath is a symptom of diabetic ketoacidosis, characterized by a fruity or acetone-like odor on the breath.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698959</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Missed Meal">
  <data key="d0">Missed Meal</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A missed meal can trigger hypoglycemia in patients using insulin, especially if the insulin dose is not adjusted accordingly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698960</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unplanned Physical Exertion">
  <data key="d0">Unplanned Physical Exertion</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Unplanned physical exertion increases glucose uptake and can lead to hypoglycemia in insulin-treated patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698961</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insulin Antibodies">
  <data key="d0">Insulin Antibodies</data>
  <data key="d1">substance</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin antibodies are circulating proteins that may bind insulin, affecting its distribution and potentially altering pharmacokinetics when present.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulin antibodies are circulating proteins that can bind to insulin, potentially affecting its distribution and action.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43208e8857e43453277beecb7a6c55d5</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protease">
  <data key="d0">Protease</data>
  <data key="d1">enzyme</data>
  <data key="d2">Protease refers to enzymes such as insulin protease that degrade insulin in the body during metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protein Disulfide Isomerase">
  <data key="d0">Protein Disulfide Isomerase</data>
  <data key="d1">enzyme</data>
  <data key="d2">Protein disulfide isomerase is an enzyme potentially involved in the metabolic breakdown of insulin.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698962</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Novolin® R FlexPen®">
  <data key="d0">Novolin® R FlexPen®</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Novolin® R FlexPen® is a pre-filled insulin delivery device containing human insulin at a concentration of 100 UI/mL, designed for the treatment of diabetes in both adults and children. It is primarily used for subcutaneous injection, though some descriptions indicate it may also be administered via intramuscular or intravenous routes under appropriate medical supervision. The device is classified as a rapid-acting insulin medication, supporting glycemic control through timely insulin delivery.

The Novolin® R FlexPen® features a user-friendly design that allows for precise dose selection, with the ability to adjust doses from 1 to 60 units in 1-unit increments. It includes a dose selector and an injection button to facilitate accurate and controlled administration. As a pre-filled, disposable system, it requires the use of disposable needles for each injection to maintain hygiene and ensure safe use. The device is intended for subcutaneous use in most clinical settings, aligning with standard insulin administration practices.

Novolin® R FlexPen® is a regulated medical product that requires a prescription and is subject to oversight by health authorities to ensure safety and efficacy. Proper labeling, storage, and handling are essential to prevent medication errors and maintain the stability of the insulin formulation. While most indications emphasize subcutaneous delivery, the inclusion of intravenous and intramuscular routes in some descriptions may reflect specific clinical scenarios, such as hospital settings, where alternative administration methods are medically justified. Overall, the device is designed to provide a safe, reliable, and convenient method for insulin delivery in diabetes management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-849980827afbc1e3097aed3d19640efe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b93b5769e8df2e4aa6c2c1dc9e65fd85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1954b944b779698ff7d67b256199d202&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ee5ffd422bf5c38703d11eefbcff6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9fa1ee045093083e543d672f1309db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc3409d91fe33592f741a0002d70749c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-581b6c5da621e9091266d2b0e1919308&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1229dbbfb74d991d522a8d53f9383cdc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27ae5fe50adf25db0be3b8cdde6282e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2e44a4ca961f1a97305607fa263ed91</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778698964</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tampa">
  <data key="d0">Tampa</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Tampa is the cap of the Novolin® R FlexPen® used to protect the device when not in use.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b93b5769e8df2e4aa6c2c1dc9e65fd85</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf</data>
