</node>
<node id="Patrones Basales">
  <data key="d0">Patrones Basales</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Patrones Basales refers to the programmed insulin delivery rates that vary throughout the day to match the body's basal insulin needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Patrones Basales are predefined insulin delivery profiles that determine the rate of basal insulin infusion over 24 hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63644bff2af5cc96a50e636bff8e339b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b28f926a642d5f692433ed41fafafa4</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778700885</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Glucosa">
  <data key="d0">Glucosa</data>
  <data key="d1">substance</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucosa is a simple sugar that serves as the primary energy source for the body's cells and is commonly referred to as blood sugar. It is a natural substance that enters the bloodstream following the consumption of food, particularly carbohydrates, and must be regulated to maintain metabolic balance. The levels of glucosa in the blood are tightly controlled by hormones, primarily insulin, which facilitates the uptake of glucosa into cells to produce energy, and glucagón, which helps raise blood sugar levels when they are too low. This hormonal regulation is essential for sustaining stable energy supply and overall metabolic health.

Glucosa levels are frequently monitored in individuals with diabetes to assess and manage their condition effectively. Monitoring typically occurs at various times throughout the day, including before meals, at bedtime, during the night, and upon waking, to capture fluctuations and ensure proper control. Devices such as the MiniMed™ 640G system track glucosa levels in milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL), enabling real-time management of diabetes. Effective management often involves a combination of insulin administration, dietary adjustments, and continuous glucose monitoring to prevent complications associated with hyperglycemia or hypoglycemia. Collectively, glucosa plays a central role in both normal physiological function and the clinical management of metabolic disorders like diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63644bff2af5cc96a50e636bff8e339b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd7b4fa0ccd15d540a9299e77dbf1dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51d265f691032645cfa3e43210043f3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ecb158d437bb3657976e38d9b2e33dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6900fc7e5ef921c29efe412333f2e7ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b47e3679b82b031ef2f4cb55f5d1ca61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0fdf7fe6f512616144e110952e56f02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e15921d1b7d6415709a3d0c795cefc3c</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778704185</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Células">
  <data key="d0">Células</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Células are biological units in the body that require glucose for energy, facilitated by insulin.&lt;SEP&gt;Células are biological units in the body that require glucose, facilitated by insulin, to function and receive energy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-63644bff2af5cc96a50e636bff8e339b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6900fc7e5ef921c29efe412333f2e7ec</data>
