<node id="Bolus Wizard">
  <data key="d0">Bolus Wizard</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Bolus Wizard is a feature developed by Medtronic MiniMed, Inc., and is a registered trademark of the company, integrated into its insulin pump systems such as the MiniMed 670G, MiniMed 770G, and MiniMed 780G. Designed to assist individuals with diabetes in managing insulin delivery, Bolus Wizard functions as a decision-support tool that calculates precise insulin bolus doses by synthesizing user inputs and personalized medical settings. It operates primarily in Manual mode and must be enabled and properly configured with individualized parameters to function, including carbohydrate-to-insulin ratio, insulin sensitivity factor (correction factor), target blood glucose levels, and active insulin time. The system uses current blood glucose readings—often obtained from a connected glucose meter or continuous glucose monitoring system—and estimated carbohydrate intake to generate tailored insulin dose recommendations for both mealtime and glucose correction needs.

The Bolus Wizard enhances dosing accuracy by incorporating insulin on board (also known as active insulin), which refers to the residual insulin still effective from previous boluses, thereby preventing insulin stacking and reducing the risk of hypoglycemia. It supports the combination of meal boluses, which address carbohydrate consumption, and correction boluses, which bring elevated glucose levels back into the target range. The feature can calculate and deliver boluses using standard or dual-wave (extended) bolus patterns, although it cannot deliver correction doses as square wave boluses. While highly effective in automating dose calculations, Bolus Wizard has limitations: it does not account for insulin administered via manual injections when calculating active insulin, and in certain models like the MiniMed 780G, it does not retain active insulin data after the pump enters storage mode.

Overall, Bolus Wizard serves as a critical component of modern insulin pump therapy, streamlining diabetes management by integrating real-time glucose data, dietary intake, and personalized insulin kinetics into a cohesive, automated dosing recommendation. By reducing the cognitive load on users and minimizing calculation errors, it promotes safer and more individualized insulin therapy.</data>
