<node id="Insulina Activa">
  <data key="d0">Insulina Activa</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulina Activa refers to the amount of insulin from previous bolus doses that remains active or effective in the body and continues to lower blood glucose levels. This residual insulin is a critical factor in diabetes management, particularly for individuals using insulin pumps, as it influences current and future insulin dosing decisions. Insulina Activa is dynamically calculated based on the pharmacokinetics of the insulin used and decreases over time as the insulin is metabolized.

This metric is displayed on the home screen of insulin pump systems such as the MiniMed™ 640G, providing users with real-time information about the ongoing insulin effect. Its primary purpose is to prevent insulin stacking, a potentially dangerous situation where overlapping doses of insulin lead to excessive insulin action and increase the risk of hypoglycemia. By showing how much insulin is still active, the system enables more accurate calculation of corrective or mealtime bolus doses, as the Insulina Activa is subtracted from new dose recommendations.

Insulina Activa is consistently described as a component integrated into current dosing algorithms, ensuring that healthcare providers and patients account for the lingering effects of prior bolus administrations. Whether presented in Spanish or English, the concept emphasizes the importance of considering residual insulin activity to maintain safe and effective glycemic control. Overall, Insulina Activa serves as a safety and precision tool in insulin therapy, promoting informed decision-making in real time.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Activa is the remaining insulin from bolus doses that is still active in reducing blood glucose levels over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Activa is the remaining active insulin from previous bolus doses, subtracted from the current corrective dose to avoid overdose.&lt;SEP&gt;Insulina Activa refers to the remaining active insulin from previous bolus doses that is factored into current insulin dosage calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edf22db77193377cf626b08881757226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fac8b3f8df4c710a4e2c9640b886b16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4f908910b79b1f9ccf095fd5fb4035&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89156e5c9ff284920f8f122774e1ee34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1064c42775365801d8ca4df533dcda0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95f095f73e1ec982aa57fd66798ffaec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ecb158d437bb3657976e38d9b2e33dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7fe03750967000c5e6da4698616f3cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1161d90ae3c3dfe3fc54cc15351e9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed9acad938fa761961897e399e22d5c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b83b1b837df0b960758dfd02d42d69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d74e338f7a37de66d4b2268a49021c4e</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/887d88.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778704137</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GS">
  <data key="d0">GS</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">GS refers to blood glucose level, typically measured in milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL), and serves as a critical parameter in diabetes management. It is commonly assessed using a blood glucose meter and provides essential data for monitoring glucose control in diabetic patients. These measurements are frequently taken throughout the day to detect fluctuations and ensure glucose levels remain within a safe range.

GS values are directly utilized in determining insulin dosage, particularly in the calculation of bolus insulin required before meals or to correct high blood glucose levels. The readings are often input into insulin dosage algorithms or displayed on insulin pumps, where they inform real-time treatment decisions. As such, accurate and timely GS measurements are vital for effective glycemic control and the prevention of complications associated with hyperglycemia or hypoglycemia. Multiple descriptions consistently emphasize the role of GS as an input for insulin dosage calculations, reinforcing its central function in both manual and automated insulin delivery systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edf22db77193377cf626b08881757226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-191c82775d35bbc705205ed493c43c7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4f908910b79b1f9ccf095fd5fb4035&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f2cf8a591a83e4436ab6a76c2d30e2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed9acad938fa761961897e399e22d5c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b83b1b837df0b960758dfd02d42d69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d74e338f7a37de66d4b2268a49021c4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e15921d1b7d6415709a3d0c795cefc3c</data>
