<node id="Piston">
  <data key="d0">Piston</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The piston is located inside the pump and acts like a syringe plunger, pushing insulin from the reservoir through the tubing and into the body.|&lt;SEP&gt;The Piston is a mechanical component within the pump that moves in the reservoir compartment and returns to its start position during the rewinding phase.&lt;SEP&gt;The piston is located inside the pump and pushes up on the reservoir to move insulin through the tubing and into the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The Piston is located in the reservoir compartment of the pump and pushes insulin through the tubing by engaging the reservoir.&lt;SEP&gt;The part of the insulin pump that engages the reservoir and moves insulin through the tubing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-682b7d7aa57e01aca00a1d4393db7bd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7908c9940fceb874990f2aa93b53b668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a6d7d1adf71e2872a4ddf28d3170247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd82ff17ab8577d82477ed97f94075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90de561bcfc1f3544a690722c75e6f2</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778707313</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mini-Computer">
  <data key="d0">Mini-Computer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The mini-computer inside the insulin pump controls the piston and administers insulin in very small doses, as low as 0.025 units.|</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-682b7d7aa57e01aca00a1d4393db7bd9</data>
  <data key="d4">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/231037.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778700902</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Battery">
  <data key="d0">Battery</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The insulin pump is powered by an AA battery, which can be lithium, alkaline, or rechargeable. A new or fully charged battery should always be used.|&lt;SEP&gt;The battery powers the insulin pump, and its low level is indicated by a red bar; a completely black icon with a red outline indicates immediate replacement is needed.&lt;SEP&gt;Battery refers to the power source of the pump, with its charge level indicated by a status icon that changes color and fill level as it depletes.&lt;SEP&gt;The pump requires one AA battery, such as a lithium, alkaline, or nickel-metal hydride battery, to function.&lt;SEP&gt;The battery powers the pump and must be replaced when low to ensure uninterrupted insulin delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Battery powers the insulin pump and has associated alerts for failure, incompatibility, and replacement.</data>
