<edge source="Insulin" target="Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d8">9.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The relation between Hypoglycemia and Insulin is primarily characterized by the risk that insulin therapy poses in inducing abnormally low blood sugar levels, a condition known as hypoglycemia. This adverse effect is the most frequent complication associated with insulin treatment and arises due to various factors related to dosage, administration technique, and individual patient conditions. Excessive insulin dosage, whether in absolute terms or relative to a patient’s metabolic needs, directly contributes to hypoglycemia by driving blood glucose levels below normal thresholds.

Improper administration practices significantly increase this risk. For instance, injecting insulin into muscle instead of subcutaneous tissue can accelerate insulin absorption, leading to a rapid drop in blood glucose. Similarly, sudden changes in insulin injection sites—from areas with lipohypertrophy or altered absorption to unaffected tissue—can unpredictably increase insulin uptake, necessitating careful blood glucose monitoring and potential dose adjustments. These technical factors underscore the importance of proper training in insulin self-administration to minimize hypoglycemic events.

Beyond physical health implications, hypoglycemia induced by insulin can impair cognitive functions such as concentration, alertness, and reaction time. This poses significant safety concerns, particularly during activities requiring acute mental focus, such as driving or operating heavy machinery. The severity and frequency of hypoglycemia are further influenced by patient-specific factors, including diet, physical activity, comorbidities, and the target level of glycemic control. Therefore, effective insulin management requires a personalized approach that balances optimal glucose control with the minimization of hypoglycemic episodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Too much insulin delivery may cause hypoglycemia, a condition of abnormally low blood glucose levels.</data>
  <data key="d10">adverse effect,adverse reaction,blood glucose regulation,blood sugar control,cause,dosage effect,dosage imbalance,medical risk,metabolic disturbance,metabolic effect,overdose risk,risk factor</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-84c3145631d64a0c7b9ea21dfb9c8275&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e490a7e963a20e2221a8097b50073543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00bac63e27caeb094fe802c0bfe00a44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc07da1fd30cc15e0022830f701a9c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62a6c42a674563e36382f398efac64a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0ee5ffd422bf5c38703d11eefbcff6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbd230d2ccb1901e088f3bb5714424fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3000f8880af4ef448483b353db654f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-851fca8611c248b7f7b991f6dd4abbe2</data>
  <data key="d12">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Aplicação de insulina/Documento sem título.docx&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_levemir.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/Bulas/bula_regular novonordiks.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/41d0f4.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1778708094</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
  <data key="d15">Hypoglycemia</data>
