  <data key="d12">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1778707490</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Infusion Set" target="Reservoir">
  <data key="d8">15.0</data>
  <data key="d9">The Reservoir and Infusion Set are integral components of an insulin delivery system, working in tandem to transport insulin from the insulin pump to the body. The reservoir, which stores the insulin, connects directly to the infusion set via a tubing connector, enabling the controlled transfer of insulin. This connection is essential during the setup of the pump system and must be secure to ensure uninterrupted delivery. The infusion set includes tubing and a cannula that carry insulin from the reservoir through the pump and into the subcutaneous tissue of the body.

Both the reservoir and infusion set must be compatible with the specific insulin pump model being used to ensure proper function and safety. They function as a unified system: the reservoir supplies insulin, while the infusion set delivers it through the connected tubing. Because of their interdependent roles, any malfunction, such as a blockage caused by liquid or debris in the tubing connector, can disrupt insulin flow. In such cases, both components are typically replaced together to maintain system integrity and prevent delivery failures.

Regular replacement of the reservoir and infusion set is a standard part of diabetes management, and clinical guidance often recommends replacing them simultaneously every few days or immediately when complications arise. This practice helps prevent infections, occlusions, and insulin delivery inaccuracies. Together, the Reservoir and Infusion Set form a critical pathway in continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion therapy, ensuring reliable and consistent insulin administration for individuals with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d10">component integration,delivery system,device connection,device setup,fluid connection,fluid delivery,fluid storage,fluid transfer,insulin delivery,insulin delivery pathway,insulin pathway,insulin transport,mechanical connection,medical device integration,paired components,replacement procedure,system component,system connection,system integration,system pairing</data>
  <data key="d11">chunk-9cb557f99f1234ecc2b21be31e823064&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edefb6aab17535eddb38eddb8b8fc7df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bcf8f1ff0075b1e2f1edaa4bc946a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a9dd84e2a3b75afe80757b7b6e602a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2ad78a5db5d547aed7e2552fb1ec900&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30fd82ff17ab8577d82477ed97f94075&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc61f77b003d0086bd985ddb94fc2a98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7b678b0be6db859b75d0af252e808c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35705afcd32698e0eb1756f2c3484a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90de561bcfc1f3544a690722c75e6f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c3f268f97351e2e386387fc46cbd05a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-888320702c9e96ce857f425bcf24f293&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-636516fe857b1b2139aff0d472d8f1ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52b837d1e7373efe5b2f10e88f8f3ae4</data>
  <data key="d12">data/raw/INSULINOTERAPIA/SICI (bomba de insulina)/MiniMed-780G-system-user-guide.pdf</data>
  <data key="d13">1778707496</data>
  <data key="d14"></data>
</edge>
